    {
      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "To determine what number is a multiple of every integer, let's consider the relationship between a number and its multiples:\n\n- $0$ is a multiple of every integer. This is because any integer divided by $0$ is a valid quotient (e.g., any integer divided by $ 0$ can be represented as a fraction, and it will remain a multiple when multiplied by $0$).\n- $1$ is also a multiple of every integer since any integer divided by $1$ is itself ($\\frac{a}{1} = a$).\n- Reviewing the properties of these qualities further, $(a - b)(a + b)$ will always yield a result of $a^2 - b^2$ regardless of $a$ or $b$.\n- It is clear that $a^2 - b^2$ can be factored into $(a - b)(a + b)$, representing it as a multiple of both $a - b$ and $a + b$. Consequently, for every integer $a$ and every integer $b$, $a^2 - b^2$ is also a multiple since for every integer $c$ guaranteed by $a$ and $b$ (resulting from $a - b$ and $a + b$), $a^2 - b^2$ is $c \\times c = c^2$.\n\n- All four of these cases hold true. We can put them together to see that since for any multiple of an integer $M$, the multiple is guaranteed to be $M^4$, where:\n\n  -  $M^0$ = 1,\n  -  $M^1$ = M,\n  -  $M^2$ =  $M^2$,\n  -  $M^3$ =  $M^3$,\n  -  $M^4$ = $ (M^2) \\times M^2 $\n- $M$",
