    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "A multiple of every integer is a number that is divisible by every integer.\n\nAny integer can be divided evenly by any other integer. Consider two integers, say 2 and 3. 2 is divisible by 3 (3 times 2 equals 6), and 3 is divisible by 2 (2 times 3 equals 6). So, 6 is a multiple of both 2 and 3.\n\nWe can consider smaller numbers, like 1 and 4. 1 is divisible by 4 (4 times 1 equals 4), and 4 is divisible by 1 (1 times 4 equals 4). So, 4 is a multiple of both 1 and 4.\n\nWe can generalize this reasoning for all integers. 47 is divisible by both 47 and 1 (1 times 47 equals 47), which means 47 is a multiple of all integers.\n\nThus, the number that is a multiple of every integer is \\boxed{47}.",
