    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "Let $N$ be the number we are looking for. We want $N$ to be divisible by every integer, so $N$ must divide $2k+2$ for all integers $k$.\nThis means that for any integer $n$, we must have $N|2n+2$, or $N|\\frac{2n+2}{n}$.\nSince $N\\mid 2$, $N$ must also (divisibly) divide $2$. Similarly, if $N\\mid n$, then $N\\mid 2n$ and $N\\mid 2$.\nTherefore, $N\\mid n$ for all integers $n$. Since any integer is divisible by $1$, this means that $N\\mid 1$, which implies $N=1$.\n$\\boxed{1}$",
